### Findings

- **F02-1** [major] `docs/work-mode.md:74`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:519`, `.collab/c3-work/work/01-ZAI-claude-glm-5.3/diff.txt:1` - Alternative 2 (sighted tester 'gets the author's diff') has no material to work with: the record holds no full diff (diff.txt is git status before/after plus diff --stat only) and a worker may not run git itself, so the tester can never actually see the author's code change without new c3 code. Trigger: Implementing --role tests --of <work-id> as described in the brief's alternative 2. Evidence: read-code: diff.txt is specified as git status before/after plus git diff --stat, computed by c3.; read-code: The real record contains status lines and a stat summary only - no hunks, no code.; read-code: Bash(git:*) is refused on the command line; a worker never runs git. Verify: Grep the work record writer for any code that emits a full git diff; there is none, so a sighted tester cannot be fed the change without new c3 code. Remedy: When a run is linked as tester, c3 computes the full diff of files_changed against baseline_hashes into a sanitized patch.diff in the record (or inlines it in the prompt), under the same redactor as report.md.
- **F02-2** [major] `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:869`, `docs/work-mode.md:160` - There is no way to send an instruction back into a finished author session: --resume refuses any run not in quota/timeout/waiting_quota, so the coordinator's position 'fixes go back to the same author session' is unimplementable today and every fix iteration is a fresh run that re-reads everything. Trigger: A cross-model test fails and the coordinator wants the author to fix the code in the same session. Evidence: read-code: claim_resume refuses unless status is quota | timeout | waiting_quota; a done run is explicitly 'not resumable'.; read-code: The documented resume form is quota/timeout recovery with a fixed nudge prompt, not a new instruction. Verify: Run c3 work --task c3-work --resume 01-ZAI-claude-glm-5.3 and observe the 'state done, not resumable' refusal. Remedy: Add a --continue <id> form (claude --resume / codex thread / muse session) that relaunches a done run's session with a new prompt, recorded as a new linked run or attempt; or accept fresh runs and say so in the design.
- **F02-3** [major] `docs/work-mode.md:41`, `docs/work-mode.md:234` - The brief's 'a path guard limits it to test files' cannot be a pre-run containment on any current engine: claude runs acceptEdits with no path allowlist, codex's boundary is workspace-write, muse is unrestricted - only a post-hoc check of files_changed against a declared scope is real. Trigger: Any implementation of the path scope in alternatives 2 or 3 as launch-time enforcement. Evidence: read-code: The claude child gets write tools under --permission-mode acceptEdits; no path restriction exists in the argv.; read-code: codex is sandboxed to the whole workspace; muse writes with shell ON; --allow applies to claude only. Verify: Inspect the three engine argv builders for any path-scoping flag; none exists. Remedy: Make the v1 guard post-hoc: files_changed (already computed against baseline_hashes) must be a subset of the declared scope, else the run ends with a scope flag and non-zero exit; never advertise it as containment.
- **F02-4** [minor] `docs/work-mode.md:113`, `.collab/c3-work/work/01-ZAI-claude-glm-5.3/diff.txt:1` - The validity gate 'tests must fail on the base commit' is undefined in this repo: C3 never commits and baselines are routinely dirty, so the author's change exists in no commit to check out - the gate must reverse-apply the author's patch in a scratch worktree instead, must distinguish compile-fail (weak evidence, unavoidable for new APIs) from assertion-fail, and costs two serialized full builds under the -j2/OOM rule. Trigger: Running the alternative-4 gate on any task whose tree was dirty before the author ran. Evidence: read-code: The first real run's baseline git status shows a dirty tree (modified src files, untracked work dirs) - there is no clean base commit to test.; read-code: The record's baseline is a status list plus content hashes, not a commit ref. Verify: Show a git ref that contains the author's pre-change state for a task with a dirty baseline; none exists. Remedy: Specify the gate as: in a scratch worktree, reverse-apply the author's sanitized patch, run the tester's tests (-j 2, alone), require a runtime assertion failure (compile failure recorded as weak/pass-with-note), then pass on the full tree.
- **F02-5** [minor] `crates/c3-core/src/roster.rs:66` - c3 cannot enforce 'different family' because the roster has no family field - entries carry provider label, model, engine, roles, plan and ext; the family is only implied by label strings, and the one pairing that should be refused outright (GLM with GLM-flash: same family, same z.ai plan and quota) is detectable only via the shared label/plan. Trigger: Implementing same-family refusal for author/tester pairings. Evidence: read-code: RosterEntry has no family key; labels like ZAI-claude and byteplus-claude encode vendors by convention only.; read-code: The z.ai workers share one endpoint/plan. Verify: Grep the roster schema and validate_roster for 'family'; it is absent. Remedy: Warn (not refuse) in v1; refuse only same provider label (a shared plan means shared quota); add ext.family later only if enforcement proves worth it.
- **F02-6** [minor] `crates/c3-core/src/roster.rs:1507`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:1751` - A tests/-only scope conflicts with the repo's dominant test idiom: most tests are inline #[cfg(test)] modules inside src files, so a scoped tester is forced into integration-style tests, and an author barred from tests/ can still add or weaken inline cfg(test) tests invisibly to any path-based scope check. Trigger: Applying the alternative-3 author scope ('may not edit the test files') to a change whose natural tests are unit tests in src. Evidence: read-code: Hundreds of lines of inline unit tests at the foot of roster.rs - the house style.; read-code: Same inline-test idiom in the work module. Verify: Count #[cfg(test)] occurrences under src/ vs files under tests/ in this repo; src dominates. Remedy: Declare the tester's scope as tests/** plus files the brief names; state plainly in the design that inline cfg(test) edits are invisible to a path scope and accepted; revisit a diff-aware scope only if abuse shows up.
- **F02-7** [note] `docs/work-mode.md:253` - Prompt injection through author-produced content lands worst on a muse tester: the muse worker requires --unrestricted (shell ON, no sandbox), so injected instructions in a diff or report it reads can drive arbitrary shell commands, unlike a claude-engine tester confined to the cargo allow list. Trigger: A sighted tester run on the muse engine reading an author diff or report containing instructions. Evidence: read-code: The muse worker needs --unrestricted because --disable-sandbox has no shell containment; the allow list does not apply to it.; inferred: The tester consumes untrusted-adjacent content by design in alternatives 2-3. Verify: Confirm no allow-list or sandbox applies to a muse work run on this host (the design says none does). Remedy: Keep tester runs on the claude engine under the default allow list whenever the tester consumes author output; record the injection caveat in the design; sanitize the patch the tester receives.

